Two things are happening at once. Saruman turns every noncreature spell you cast into +1/+1 counters on a single Army equal to that spell's mana value — so the interaction you were holding up anyway assembles a lethal creature for free. And the spell list itself is pointed squarely at the decks Grixis usually loses to: white prison and green ramp. Gloom, Perish, Dystopia, Hibernation and Filter Out are not filler, they are the reason the deck exists.

# The commander
Saruman, the White Hand :: every noncreature spell amasses Orcs equal to its mana value, and your Orcs and Goblins have ward {2}. A Cyclonic Rift is seven counters. A Mana Drain is two. The Army is your clock and your blocker and it never needed a card slot of its own.
Orcish Siegemaster :: your Orcs and Goblins gain trample, and it attacks for the greatest power on your board. It is what stops the Army being chump-blocked forever.
Lazotep Plating :: amass one AND hexproof for you and everything you control. It is a counterspell for removal, a fog for a targeted wrath, and a Saruman trigger.
Slip Out the Back :: one mana to phase the Army out of a wrath, plus a counter.
Deflecting Swat :: free with your commander out.

# The other spell payoffs
Ovika, Enigma Goliath :: X hasty Goblins for every noncreature spell, where X is its mana value. Alongside Saruman, one big spell is a board and a lethal Army in the same breath — and the Goblins get ward from Saruman and trample from Orcish Siegemaster.
Sedgemoor Witch :: a Pest for every instant and sorcery, with menace and ward on the body.
Archmage Emeritus :: a card for every instant and sorcery you cast or copy.
Guttersnipe :: two damage to each opponent per instant and sorcery.
Birgi, God of Storytelling :: red mana on every spell you cast, which is how the big turns get bigger. The back face turns dead cards into two impulse draws.

# Beating white
Gloom :: white spells cost {3} more, and activated abilities of white enchantments cost {3} more. Against a prison or Voltron deck this is close to a lock, and the second clause specifically answers the enchantments Grixis cannot destroy.
Dystopia :: every player sacrifices a green or white permanent every upkeep. You have none, so it is entirely one-sided, and the cumulative upkeep is paid in life.
Deathmark :: one mana for a green or white creature.
Debt to the Kami :: they exile a creature OR an enchantment of their choice. Edicts are how Grixis answers a hexproof enchantment.
Pharika's Libation :: the same, at instant speed, as a sacrifice.
Extract the Truth :: strips a creature, enchantment or planeswalker from their hand, or forces an enchantment sacrifice.

# Beating green
Perish :: destroys every green creature on the board. Against an elf or ramp deck it is a one-sided wrath for three mana.
Hibernation :: returns every green PERMANENT to hand — creatures, enchantments, mana auras and all. It undoes an entire green deck's turn.
Ashiok, Dream Render :: opponents cannot search their libraries at all. Fetchlands, tutors, Cultivate and every green ramp spell stop working.
Opposition Agent :: flash him in and you control their search. Everything they find is yours.

# Beating stax itself
Filter Out :: returns ALL noncreature, nonland permanents to their owners' hands. Every prison enchantment, every mana rock, every Equipment at the table, gone at instant speed — and it is a four-counter Saruman trigger on top.
Rushing River :: bounce one permanent, or two if you kick it. Instant speed answers to a lock piece.
Cyclonic Rift :: overloaded, the same effect aimed only at them, and it amasses seven.
Brotherhood's End :: three damage to every creature and planeswalker, or every cheap artifact destroyed. Pick whichever half the table is built on.
Imprisoned in the Moon :: turns a commander, a land or a planeswalker into a colourless land they cannot recast.
Reality Shift :: exile a creature and leave them a face-down 2/2.
Feed the Swarm :: the rare black spell that kills an enchantment outright.
Chaos Warp :: the answer to anything at all, once.

# Counterspells that build the Army
Counterspell :: two counters and the answer.
Mana Drain :: two counters, and the mana casts the next spell.
Mana Sculpt :: three counters, and with a Wizard out — which is nearly always — it refunds their mana to you.
Force of Negation :: free on their turn.
An Offer You Can't Refuse :: one mana for anything noncreature.
Muddle the Mixture :: a counterspell, or transmute for any three-drop — Sedgemoor Witch, Ovika's setup, or Dimir Machinations.
Baral, Chief of Compliance :: cheaper spells, and a loot every time you counter something.
Flame of Anor :: with a Wizard out, take two of three modes. Draw two and kill a creature, usually.

# Mana and tutors
Dark Ritual :: three black on turn one.
Seething Song :: five red for three.
Rite of Flame :: cheap acceleration that is also a Saruman trigger.
Goblin Electromancer :: instants and sorceries cost one less.
Nightscape Familiar :: the same for blue and red, on a regenerating body.
Demonic Tutor :: any card.
Grim Tutor :: the second copy.
Merchant Scroll :: any blue instant, which usually means a counterspell or Filter Out.
Dimir Machinations :: transmute for a three-drop, or strip their top three cards.
Ringsight :: any card sharing a colour with a legend you control.
Impulse :: dig four at instant speed.
Pieces of the Puzzle :: two instants or sorceries from the top five.

# What to keep in an opening hand
Keep interaction and mana, not threats. The Army IS the threat, and it only exists because you cast spells — a hand of counterspells and removal is a hand of Saruman triggers.
Read the table before you keep the hosers. Gloom, Perish, Dystopia and Hibernation are dead cards against a mono-black or artifact table and game-winning against a green or white one. Against the wrong pod they are your worst four cards, so spend them as Saruman fodder rather than holding them.
Saruman, the White Hand :: four mana, and every turn he is out is counters you are not getting. He is a 2/5 that blocks well, so he is safer to deploy early than most commanders of his cost.
Ovika, Enigma Goliath :: hold her until you have a big spell to follow with. Landing her and immediately overloading Cyclonic Rift is seven Goblins with haste, seven counters on the Army, and usually the game.
